namespace fast {
/** @page python-tutorial-gui Making graphical user interfaces (GUIs) with FAST in Python
@tableofcontents

Introduction
--------------------------

FAST uses [Qt](https://qt.io) for making graphical user interfaces (GUIs).
If you want to make an advanced GUI with FAST in Python you should use PySide2 along with FAST, see the [bottom of this tutorial](#advanced-guis-with-pyside2).
However, if you just want to make a simple GUI you can also use the builtin widgets in FAST, which are simple wrappers
of some of Qt's widgets and dialogs.
A **widget** is a graphical element for user interaction, such as a button or a window etc.
This tutorial will start by showing how to use these widgets in FAST to make simple GUIs quickly.

Popup message
---------------------
Let's start simple, here is how you can show a simple message in a window on screen using the showMessage() function:

@code{.py}
import fast
fast.showMessage('Hello!')
@endcode

You can also add a title to the message:

@code{.py}
import fast
fast.showMessage('Hello!', 'My first message')
@endcode


File dialog
---------------------
Here is how you can ask the user to select a single image file using the showFileDialog() function, then open and display the image:
@code{.py}
import fast
filename = fast.showFileDialog()
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(filename)
fast.display2D(importer)
@endcode

This dialog also supports selecting multiple files, directories, and file for saving which can be configured using the function's arguments.

Button
---------------------

Buttons can be used to trigger an action when the user presses them by using callback functions.
In the example below, a ButtonWidget with the text "Select image" is added to the window.
When the user presses the button, the openFile function is called which opens the file dialog, and gives the filename
string to the ImageFileImporter. The importer is connected to the ImageRenderer, executed and finally we need to reset
the 2D view, i.e. reset the camera of the view using Window::resetViews().

@code{.py}
import fast

# Callback function for when button is pressed
def openFile(x):
    global renderer, window
    filename = fast.showFileDialog()
    if filename:
        importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(filename)
        renderer.connect(importer).run()
        window.resetViews()

openButton = fast.ButtonWidget('Select image', False, fast.ButtonCallback(openFile))
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer.create()
window = fast.SimpleWindow2D.create()\
    .connect(renderer)\
    .connect(openButton)
window.run()
@endcode

Text
---------------------

Text can be added to the GUI using the TextWidget which uses Qt's QLabel object.
This widget supports basic HTML tags for styling such as `<h1>, <h2>, <strong>` etc.
The widget also supports variables in the text using `{variable_name}`.
In the example below we expand on the previous example, by adding a TextWidget to the GUI
which shows the filename of the opened file.
The TextWidget is placed in top of the GUI, while the ButtonWidget is placed at the bottom by setting the position
in the connect() method to fast.WidgetPosition_TOP and fast.WidgetPosition_BOTTOM.

@code{.py}
import fast

def openFile(x):
    global renderer, window, title
    filename = fast.showFileDialog()
    if filename:
        title.setVariable('filename', filename)
        importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(filename)
        renderer.connect(importer).run()
        window.resetViews()

title = fast.TextWidget('<h1>Image Viewer</h1>{filename}')
title.setVariable('filename', '')
openButton = fast.ButtonWidget('Select image', False, fast.ButtonCallback(openFile))
renderer = fast.ImageRenderer.create()
window = fast.SimpleWindow2D.create()\
    .connect(renderer)\
    .connect(title, fast.WidgetPosition_TOP)\
    .connect(openButton, fast.WidgetPosition_BOTTOM)
window.run()
@endcode

Slider
---------------------
Sliders can be used to set numeric values within a range, for instance to set parameters for some algorithm.
Here is an example with SliderWidgets to control the Non Local Means filter, including a button to toggle the filter on and off
by disabling the renderer:
@code{.py}
import fast

importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_0.mhd')

nlm = fast.NonLocalMeans.create(
        filterSize=3, # Increasing this makes it slower
        searchSize=11, # Increasing this makes it slower
        smoothingAmount=0.2,
        inputMultiplicationWeight=0.5,
).connect(importer)

renderer = fast.ImageRenderer.create()\
    .connect(importer)

rendererNLM = fast.ImageRenderer.create()\
    .connect(nlm)

sliderWidget = fast.SliderWidget('Smoothing', 0.2, 0.05, 0.8, 0.05, fast.SliderCallback(lambda x: nlm.setSmoothingAmount(x)))
filterWidget = fast.SliderWidget('Filter size', 3, 3, 19, 2, fast.SliderCallback(lambda x: nlm.setFilterSize(int(x))))
searchWidget = fast.SliderWidget('Search size', 11, 3, 19, 2, fast.SliderCallback(lambda x: nlm.setSearchSize(int(x))))
inputWidget = fast.SliderWidget('Input multiplication weight', 0.5, 0.0, 1.0, 0.05, fast.SliderCallback(lambda x: nlm.setInputMultiplicationWeight(x)))
toggleButton = fast.ButtonWidget('Toggle ON/OFF', True, fast.ButtonCallback(lambda x: rendererNLM.setDisabled(x)))
fast.SimpleWindow2D.create()\
    .connect(renderer)\
    .connect(rendererNLM)\
    .connect([sliderWidget, filterWidget, searchWidget, inputWidget, toggleButton], fast.WidgetPosition_RIGHT)\
    .run()
@endcode

@image html images/tutorials/ultrasound/simple_gui2.jpg


Input text
---------------------
Use the InputTextWidget to enable users to write and provide some text to your program:
@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/US-2D.jpg')
input = fast.InputTextWidget()
button = fast.ButtonWidget('Done', False, fast.ButtonCallback(lambda x: fast.showMessage('You wrote: ' + input.getText())))
fast.display2D(importer, widgets=[input, button])
@endcode

For multiline input text, set singleLine=False in constructor of InputTextWidget:
@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/US-2D.jpg')
input = fast.InputTextWidget(singleLine=False)
button = fast.ButtonWidget('Done', False, fast.ButtonCallback(lambda x: fast.showMessage('You wrote: ' + input.getText())))
fast.display2D(importer, widgets=[input, button])
@endcode


Options
---------------------

The OptionsWidget can be used to let the user select one of several predefined options in a dropdown button.
You can specify a title, and a placeholder text. If the placeholder text is specified, no option is selected initially.
If no placeholder text is specified, option 0 is initially selected, unless you set `selected=index`.

@code{.py}
import fast
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/US-2D.jpg')
options = fast.OptionsWidget(['Option 1', 'Option 2', 'Option 3'], 'Cool options:', 'Select an option ...', callback=fast.OptionsCallback(lambda i, x: fast.showMessage('You selected: ' + x)))
fast.display2D(importer, widgets=[options])
@endcode


Table
---------------------

Tabular data can be displayed using the TableWidget.

@code{.py}
import fast

# Make some tabular data
data = [
    ('Column 1', ['1.23', '2.3', '12.3']),
    ('Column 2', ['2.23', '1.3', '13.3']),
    ('Last Column', ['Hello!', '', 'Ok!']),
]

# Make a callback function for when user clicks on a cell in the table
def clickCallback(widget, row, column, doubleClick):
    message = f'You pressed the cell {row},{column}\n'
    message += f'Row {row} contains the following:\n'
    data = widget.getRow(row)
    for key, value in data.items():
        message += f'{key}: {value}\n'
    fast.showMessage(message)

# Setup widget and show it
widget = fast.TableWidget(data, fast.TableCallback(clickCallback))
widget.show(maximized=True)
@endcode

Playback controls
---------------------
The PlaybackWidget is useful when you want some simple GUI to play/pause/stop, scroll through the frames or change the framerate of a stream of data.
Give the RandomAccessStreamer as input to the PlaybackWidget constructor and connect it to your window:

@code{.py}
import fast

# Specify path to where the images are stored, use a # to represent the frame index
streamer = fast.ImageFileStreamer.create(
    fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/Heart/ApicalFourChamber/US-2D_#.mhd',
    framerate=20, # Specify framerate to stream data in
)

# Create playback widget and use it in the display window
widget = fast.PlaybackWidget(streamer)
fast.display2D(streamer, widgets=[widget])
@endcode

@image html images/tutorials/ultrasound/playback_widget.jpg

Styling
---------------------

You can customize the appearance of your GUI using [Qt Style Sheets (QSS)](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html) which is a subset of Cascading Style Sheets (CSS).

Here is an example on how to change the appearance of your TextWidget and ButtonWidget:
@code{.py}
importer = fast.ImageFileImporter.create(fast.Config.getTestDataPath() + 'US/US-2D.jpg')
text = fast.TextWidget('Hello!')
button = fast.ButtonWidget('This is a button', True, fast.ButtonCallback(lambda x: print('Button was pressed!')))
window = fast.display2D(image=importer, returnWindow=True, widgets=[text, button], bgcolor=fast.Color.Black())
window.setStyleSheet('''
QWidget {
    background-color: #000000;
    color: #FFFFFF;
}
QLabel {
    background-color: orange;
    color: #000000;
}
QPushButton:checked {
    background-color: green;
}
QPushButton:!checked {
    background-color: red;
}
''')
window.run()
@endcode

Style sheets can also be saved to file and loaded using Window::setStyleSheetFile():
@code{.py}
window.setStyleSheetFile('path/to/style.css')
@endcode

Advanced GUIs with PySide2 {#advanced-guis-with-pyside2}
--------------------------------------------------------
PySide2 is the python wrapper of Qt 5 and is needed to make advanced GUIs with FAST in Python.
For these examples you <b>must</b> use the same Qt version of PySide2 as used in FAST (5.15.2)
Do this with: `pip install pyside2==5.15.2.1`.
Note that PySide2 is only available for Python versions up to 3.10.

This example shows how to add Qt widgets from PySide2 to a FAST window:
@include Python/pyfast_and_pyside2.py

@image html images/examples/python/pyfast_and_pyside.jpg width=400px;

This example shows how FAST can be used with a Qt window (QWidget) from PySide2:
@include Python/pyfast_and_pyside2_custom_window.py

@image html images/examples/python/pyfast_and_pyside_custom_window.jpg width=350px;

